meta {
  name: Update Email Settings
  type: http
  seq: 13
}

patch {
  url: {{baseUrl}}/agency/email
  body: json
  auth: bearer
}

auth:bearer {
  token: {{ownerAccessToken}}
}

headers {
  Content-Type: application/json
}

body:json {
  {
    "fromName": "Texas Holdings",
    "replyTo": "service@texasholdings.com"
  }
}

tests {
  test("returns 200 OK", function () {
    expect(res.getStatus()).to.equal(200);
  });

  test("the agency name reaches the From header", function () {
    expect(res.getBody().effectiveFrom).to.contain('"Texas Holdings"');
  });

  test("still sends from the platform address", function () {
    // No verified sending domain, so the address half must stay ours. The
    // agency is surfaced through the display name and Reply-To.
    expect(res.getBody().sendingStatus).to.equal("platform");
  });
}

docs {
  # PATCH /agency/email

  Set the display name, sending mailbox and reply-to.

  **Module** — none. **Permission** — `agency:email:write`.

  **Body** — every field `clearable` (`null` removes, omitted leaves alone):
  | field         | type           | rules |
  |---------------|----------------|-------|
  | fromName      | string \| null | ≤ 60 chars. Display name in `From:` |
  | fromLocalPart | string \| null | ≤ 64 chars, `[a-z0-9._-]`. Only takes effect once a domain is verified |
  | replyTo       | string \| null | a valid email, ≤ 160 chars |

  **None of these needs verification.** `fromName` and `replyTo` carry no
  authentication requirement at all — which is the point: setting `replyTo` is
  the one-field way for an agency to get replies into their own inbox with no
  DNS work whatsoever.

  The display name is **quoted and stripped of quotes, backslashes and CRLF**
  before it reaches the header. An agency name is user-supplied text being
  interpolated into a mail header; a bare quote splits it into two addresses and
  a CRLF starts a new header. That is injection, not a formatting nit.

  **Response `200`** — the same shape as **Get Email Settings**.

  **Errors**
  - `400` — malformed local part or reply-to address.
  - `403` — caller lacks `agency:email:write`.
}
